      <ul>
        <li><strong>Curated Reputable Sources:</strong> For journalism, this includes a dynamic, internally rated list of global and national news organizations (e.g., Financial Times, WSJ, AP, Reuters), vetted monthly for factual accuracy. For finance, this includes key financial databases and APIs (SEC EDGAR, Bloomberg, FactSet). For legal, primary legal databases (Westlaw, LexisNexis). For energy and government, official regulatory databases (EPA, NRC, FERC).</li>
        <li><strong>Enterprise-Specific Knowledge:</strong> Clients can integrate their own lists of trusted local/specialized sources, internal knowledge repositories, precedent documents, or proprietary data (securely indexed within the client's isolated instance).</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Multi-Source Meta-Analysis & Sophisticated Conflict Resolution</h3>
      <p><strong>The Challenge:</strong> Even trusted sources can offer conflicting accounts, interpretations, or data points. Balanced reporting and comprehensive understanding require acknowledging these nuances.</p>
      
      <p><strong>PSQRD's Approach:</strong> PSQRD performs a robust meta-analysis, cross-referencing claims against multiple, high-quality sources. Algorithms:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Weigh evidence based on source credibility (e.g., jurisdiction/court level for legal precedent, recency for financial data, established scientific consensus for technical claims) and timeliness.</li>
        <li>Identify consensus or discrepancies.</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p>Crucially, when valid conflicting evidence is found from multiple credible sources, PSQRD doesn't force a false consensus or pick a "winner." Instead, it presents these perspectives in a formal, balanced manner, with full source attribution for each. This empowers journalists to report the full picture, lawyers to understand the breadth of legal argument, and analysts to appreciate the range of financial forecasts.</p>
      
      <h3>Transparent, Source-Traceable Output Construction – The Antithesis of the "Black Box"</h3>
      <p><strong>The Challenge:</strong> AI outputs without clear sourcing are unusable where auditability and provability are paramount. Every fact must be attributable.</p>
      
      <p><strong>PSQRD's Approach:</strong> This is where our commitment to Verifiable Zero Hallucinations culminates. The final output (e.g., a fact-check report, a research summary, a due diligence alert, answers to queries) is meticulously constructed only from the verified claims and their supporting evidence.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Radical Transparency:</strong> Every single statement, financial figure, legal citation, or policy point in the output is directly and transparently linked back to the specific source(s) – document, page, paragraph, timestamp, database entry – from which it was verified.</li>
        <li><strong>Instant Auditability:</strong> Users can click through to review the original source material instantly, allowing for immediate validation and complete editorial/professional control.</li>
